      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain quantum entanglement to me? i need to understand it for my exam but i dont really get the math part so just a simple summary would be great. also how is it different from just regular correlation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Quantum entanglement is a phenomenon where two or more particles become connected in such a way that their properties are correlated, regardless of the distance between them. It's a bit like a secret handshake between the particles, where they're \"entangled\" in a way that lets them affect each other even if they're on opposite sides of the universe. Imagine two coins, one in New York and one in Tokyo - if you flip the New York coin, the Tokyo coin will instantly flip too, even if they're separated by thousands of miles. That's basically entanglement, but with particles like electrons or photons.\n\nNow, regular correlation is like a coincidence - two events happen together, but they're not connected in any way. It's like two people meeting in a crowded room by chance. But entanglement is different because it's a fundamental property of the particles themselves, not just a chance occurrence. Think of it like a pair of twins who are connected at the hip, even if they're on opposite sides of the world. They can still affect each other, even if they're not physically touching.\n\nIn terms of math, entanglement is often described using wave functions and operators, but don't worry too much about that",
      "source": "target",
